INT. MIRROR-MAKER'S WORKSHOP - TWILIGHT

Frames, silvered glass, hanging bells. A hundred mirrors lean against the walls, each reflecting a slightly different room.

A GIRL, 12, ragged hem, carries a lantern between the mirrors. She stops before the largest, an oval taller than a door.

GIRL
Hello?

ORRIN (O.S.)
(from within the glass)
Hello yourself.

She jumps. The lantern swings. In the mirror, her reflection does not move.

GIRL
Who's there?

ORRIN (O.S.)
Mirror-keeper. Orrin. And you?

GIRL
I don't know.

ORRIN (O.S.)
Everyone knows their name.

GIRL
I forgot it. Last winter.

A pause. The reflection tilts its head.

ORRIN (O.S.)
Come closer. Let me look.

She steps forward. Presses her palm to the cool glass. The reflection mirrors it, and a pale hand on the other side lines up with hers.

ORRIN (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Tamsin.

GIRL
What?

ORRIN (O.S.)
Your name. It's Tamsin. I have it here.

The girl stares. Her eyes fill.

TAMSIN
How do you know?

ORRIN (O.S.)
Mirrors keep what people lose.

She laughs, wet and astonished.

TAMSIN
Tamsin.

ORRIN (O.S.)
Say it again.

TAMSIN
Tamsin. Tamsin.

The mirror fogs, then clears. In the glass, only her face remains, smiling.
